<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  AUG-143 positive corpus: both legal rollback declarations, on blocks that earn them.

  `unsupported`      the change is not rolled back; the corpus says so instead of a reader
                     assuming either way.
  `tracked-test`     rollback IS exercised, and the proof is this block's OWN obligation pointer
                     (runner + path + selector), which the obligation pass resolves against the
                     consuming repository: the path must exist, be tracked in git, and define the
                     selector. That is why this corpus cites a real test below.

  `spec check` reports both declarations in its `rollback` map, so a consumer reads them instead of
  guessing.
-->
<spec xmlns="urn:auger:spec:1" id="AUGER-SPEC-ROLLBACK" version="1.0.0"
      schema-version="1.0">
  <verification id="AUG-VER-ROLLBACK-UNSUPPORTED-1" kind="verification" status="active"
                version="1.0.0" applies-to="auger.spec.rollback" method="inspection"
                rollback="unsupported">
    A read-only validation pass has nothing to undo: there is no write to roll back, and saying so
    explicitly is the contract (an absent declaration would leave a consumer guessing).
  </verification>

  <verification id="AUG-VER-ROLLBACK-TESTED-1" kind="verification" status="active"
                version="1.0.0" applies-to="auger.spec.rollback" method="test"
                rollback="tracked-test"
                runner="pytest" path="tests/test_spec_rollback.py"
                selector="test_a_declared_rollback_test_is_resolved_and_visible">
    The rollback path is exercised by a tracked test, and this block names it: the obligation pass
    resolves the path, proves it is tracked in git, and proves the selector is defined there.
  </verification>
</spec>
